Microsoft Learn: Secure Azure Storage

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Secure Azure Storage
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to secure Azure Storage resources by implementing encryption, Shared Access Signatures (SAS), and customer-managed encryption keys.

This module focused on protecting sensitive enterprise data stored in Azure Storage and configuring secure access methods for both internal users and external application developers.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Storage Security

The module introduced Azure Storage security capabilities designed to protect sensitive cloud data.

Types of protected data:
Personal information
Application files
Business records
Internal company data
External developer resources
Core security goals:
Protect data confidentiality
Restrict unauthorized access
Encrypt stored data
Secure external sharing
Maintain compliance requirements

Azure Storage provides layered security controls that help organizations secure cloud workloads.

Step 2: Shared Access Signatures (SAS)

A major focus of the module was configuring Shared Access Signatures (SAS).

What SAS provides:

A SAS token grants temporary, limited access to Azure Storage resources without exposing account keys.

SAS capabilities include:
Time-limited access
Permission-based access
Resource-specific access
Secure URI generation
Types of permissions:
Permission	Description
Read	View storage data
Write	Modify storage data
Delete	Remove resources
List	View resource listings
Key concepts learned:
SAS tokens improve security by avoiding full account key sharing
SAS access can expire automatically
Permissions should follow the principle of least privilege
SAS URIs combine the resource endpoint with access parameters

This approach is commonly used when securely sharing resources with external applications or users.

Step 3: Azure Storage Encryption

The module covered Azure Storage encryption methods used to protect data at rest.

Encryption features studied:
Automatic encryption by default
Encryption for blobs, files, queues, and tables
Microsoft-managed keys
Customer-managed keys
Key lessons:
Azure automatically encrypts stored data
Encryption protects against unauthorized physical access
Encryption occurs transparently without impacting application functionality

Azure Storage encryption is a core security requirement for enterprise cloud environments.

Step 4: Customer-Managed Keys (CMK)

The training introduced customer-managed encryption keys for organizations requiring greater control over encryption.

Benefits of customer-managed keys:
Greater compliance control
Key rotation management
Full ownership of encryption keys
Centralized security management
Services involved:
Microsoft Azure Key Vault
Azure Storage Accounts
Key concepts learned:
Azure Key Vault securely stores encryption keys
Organizations can rotate or revoke keys
CMKs support regulatory and compliance requirements
Access to encryption keys should be tightly controlled

Customer-managed keys provide stronger governance for highly sensitive data.

Step 5: Improving Azure Storage Security

The module emphasized best practices for strengthening storage security.

Security recommendations included:
Best Practice	Purpose
Use SAS instead of account keys	Reduce exposure risk
Enable encryption	Protect stored data
Use RBAC	Granular access control
Restrict network access	Limit public exposure
Rotate keys regularly	Improve security hygiene
Use private endpoints	Secure network communication
Additional concepts:
Principle of least privilege
Monitoring storage access
Limiting anonymous access
Secure developer integration

Security configuration plays a major role in protecting cloud-hosted business data.

Step 6: Real-World Scenario

The learning scenario involved securing sensitive company data stored in Azure Storage for both internal employees and external application developers.

Administrative goals included:
Protecting personal information
Securing developer access
Preventing unauthorized exposure
Maintaining encrypted storage
Managing secure sharing mechanisms

This demonstrated how Azure Administrators implement practical enterprise cloud security controls.

